USENET has been doomed and/or dying for a long while.  The experiment of
"no controls" has been tried and found wanting; while my news provider
does an excellent job of eradicating most spam, not all others do, and
the most easily accessible interface for non-veterans is Google, which
removes almost none of it.  With the primary source of "new blood"
unable to effectively read the groups, that means that any who move on
to better things aren't replaced.

Couple that with USENET tending to accumulate more and more of the
Internet's dregs as time goes by (since mailing lists and webforums can 
and do ban people who start getting out of control), and you start to 
ask yourself why you're even bothering... especially when superior 
communities are available where, if the discussion isn't perhaps the 
most sophisticated, it at least isn't perpetually littered with people 
who aren't interested in discussing the subject at all.

I'm really not sure why I even bother reading here anymore, myself.  Due to
demands from the RealJob(tm) and expanding responsibilities thereof, I simply
haven't had time to work on Spork anymore, and I'm not sure when I'll be 
able to get around to it again.  I'm not going to take all the resources 
down, to be sure, but at the same time, I have to be realistic; this was 
always a 'spare time' project, and as my spare time has vanished, well... 
there you are, then, yes?

Then, too, there's an argument to be made that with Crawl, POWDER, and
other roguelikes constantly expanding and in fully active development, is 
perhaps Nethack's role now to finally join Rogue, Moria and other predecessors 
in the Elder Roguelikes' Hall of Fame?  I do wonder, but that is a
question only the players can and/or will answer.

